; biss_master.loom
; A BiSS-C and SSI master: reads an absolute position encoder, the kind on
; the motor shaft of a servo drive. MA (the master's clock) goes out on one
; pin and SLO (the encoder's data) comes in on another; on a real board each
; passes an RS-422 transceiver, and this program sees the logic side only.
;
; Written on 2026-11-04, after the hardware was complete (D-032), as a
; protocol Loom was not designed for (D-035). It needs the M2 core (pins,
; deadlines, the FIFOs), the deadline-latched pin write (SEMANTICS 6.10,
; built for clock-exact UART and WS2812 edges), here placing every MA edge
; on its tick, and the bit engine in manual mode (SHI, SEMANTICS 6.9) with
; its CRC unit, built for USB's CRC5/CRC16 and CAN's CRC15, here running
; BiSS's CRC-6 (polynomial 0x43) over the data, error and warning bits as
; they are sampled. No hardware knows BiSS or SSI: the Ack and start-bit
; search, the line-delay count, the CDS bit, the split of a frame of up to
; 40 bits into the 16-bit shift register, the inverted-CRC check, the Gray
; decode and the wait for the encoder's timeout are all firmware.
;
; Sources (tools/protomodels/biss.py has the full list and what is
; unchecked): the BiSS C frame as Beckhoff's EL5042 documentation draws it
; ("Basics of BiSS-C technology", fig. 5) and iC-Haus's BiSS C protocol
; description and application notes AN3/AN6 (CRC-6 0x43, sent inverted);
; for SSI, the SSI timing of Hengstler's and Kubler's absolute encoder
; datasheets (clock idle high, first falling edge latches, MSB first, the
; monoflop time tm after the last clock).
;
; Pins: MA = OUT0 (push-pull, to the RS-422 driver), SLO = IN0 (from the
; receiver). Both move with the assembler's --pin.
;
; Host command (INQ[0]):
;     bit 15 = 1   CONFIG: bits 5:0 the position bits n (1 .. 32), bit 8 SSI
;                  (0 BiSS-C), bit 9 GRAY (decode the position from Gray
;                  code). Nothing is clocked. After reset: BiSS-C, 24 bits,
;                  binary.
;     bit 15 = 0   READ: clock one frame.
; Result (OUTQ[0]), three words per READ:
;     status       bit 0 ERR (the encoder's nE bit was 0), bit 1 WARN (nW
;                  was 0), bit 2 CRC (the CRC-6 did not check), bit 3 NOACK
;                  (no Ack, or no start bit, in its window: frame abandoned),
;                  bit 4 BUSY (SLO did not go high within 255 ticks of the
;                  READ: the encoder is still in its timeout or absent),
;                  bit 5 the CDS bit, bits 11:8 the line delay in whole MA
;                  periods (BiSS: periods after the earliest possible Ack),
;                  bit 15 SSI. For SSI only BUSY and SSI mean anything.
;     pos low      position bits 15:0
;     pos high     position bits 31:16 (0 for n <= 16)
;
; Rate: one tick is half an MA period, MA = clk / (2 * TICK). The host sets
; TICK_INT (and TICK_FRAC) before RUN. The program is written for a tick of
; 25 clocks or more (.tick below), MA 1 MHz at 50 MHz: BiSS-C allows up to
; 10 MHz, SSI is commonly 100 kHz to 1 MHz, so this master covers SSI and
; the low end of BiSS-C. Every MA edge is a SETP ..., D staged before the
; WAITD of its tick, so both half periods are exact to the clock, which
; needs more than the checker's proof: the WAITD must commit by the edge of
; its tick, and a WAITD before it completes up to 3 clocks after its own,
; so each pair needs 5 clocks of slack (3 late, 2 to commit). At .tick 25
; the longest pairs (5 slots, 20 clocks) have exactly that. At a tick of 20
; the pairs are still proved, but an edge after a 5-slot pair lands up to
; 3 clocks late (half periods of 17 and 23 clocks measured on the model).
;
; Decided at integration (2026-11-05): the iC-Haus BiSS C protocol
; description and the SSI encoder datasheets were not read; the figures
; marked unchecked in tools/protomodels/biss.py stay so marked. Line delay
; is compensated in whole MA periods only, a stated limit, not a bug. The
; program declares .tick 25 although the checker proves tick 20, because a
; deadline-latched edge needs its WAITD committed 5 clocks before the tick.

; Frame (BiSS-C): MA idles high. The first falling edge starts the frame;
; the encoder answers the second rising edge with SLO low (Ack), holds it
; while it works, raises it for the start bit, then sends CDS, the n
; position bits MSB first, nE, nW and the CRC-6 inverted, changing SLO
; after rising MA edges. The master samples SLO once a period, 3 to 6
; clocks after each falling MA edge: half a period after the encoder's
; change on a short line, the middle of the bit cell. After the
; last bit MA stays high; the encoder holds SLO low for its timeout tTO and
; then raises it, and the next READ waits for that.
; SSI: MA idles high, the first falling edge latches the position, each
; rising edge shifts out the next bit MSB first; n + 1 clock pulses, n
; samples (after falling edges 2 .. n + 1); the encoder's monoflop time tm
; then holds SLO low.
;
; Line delay. A BiSS encoder on a long cable answers late. The master looks
; for the Ack and the start bit by level, sample after sample, so a delay
; of whole MA periods just moves the frame, and the master counts it
; (status bits 11:8). Within a period it samples at one fixed phase, so a
; delay whose fractional part brings SLO's change to within a few clocks
; of the sample point (half a period plus 3 to 6 clocks after a rising MA
; edge) is not compensated (a dedicated master
; measures the delay and moves the sample point; with one tick per half
; period this program has only the one phase).
;
; Memory: 186 words, 122 at 0x000 (thread 0's quarter) and the 64 of the
; post-processing at 0x180 (thread 3's quarter; thread 3 must not be
; started with this image). The slave (biss_slave.loom, 158 words) runs
; beside it on thread 1 from 0x080, into thread 2's quarter (to 0x11D), so
; with both loaded only threads 0 and 1 run.

        .imem   512                     ; the macro build: quarters of 128 words
        .thread 0
        .pins   MA = OUT0, SLO = IN0
        .tick   25                      ; half an MA period, at the fastest
        .equ    DEFCFG = 0x8000 | 24    ; after reset: BiSS-C, 24 bits, binary
        .equ    NACK = 16               ; samples that may pass before the Ack
        .equ    NSTART = 16             ; periods the Ack may last
        .equ    RDY = 255               ; ticks to wait for SLO high (tTO)
        .equ    RESIDUE = 0x0800        ; CRC-6 left in CRC[15:10] after a good
                                        ; frame: the CRC of six 1s, because the
                                        ; CRC goes out inverted

; Registers: r0 Ack sample counter copy, then scratch; r1 configuration; r2
; frame kind for DJNZ (1 SSI, 2 BiSS), then the last chunk; r3 16; r4, r5
; the first two chunks; r6 loop counter and CDS; r7 status (and the .csr
; scratch at start only).

start:  SETP    MA, 1                   ; MA idles high
        OEP     MA, 1                   ; (a no-op unless MA is a BIDIR pin)
        .csr    BE_PINS, (pin(SLO) << 5) | 31   ; SHI samples SLO; SHO unused
        .csr    BE_CFG, 0x0202          ; MSB first (DIR), CRC_EN; NRZ
        .csr    CRC_POLY, 0x0C00        ; x^6 + x + 1 (0x43) left-aligned
        .csr    CRC_INIT, 0
        MOV16   r1, DEFCFG

cmd:    POP     r0                      ; next command (blocks while INQ is empty)
        MOV     r6, r0
        SHLI    r6, 1                   ; C = bit 15: CONFIG
        BNC     read
        MOV     r1, r0                  ; keep it; nothing is clocked
        JMP     cmd

read:   SETD    RDY                     ; give the encoder its timeout
        WAITP   SLO, 1, T               ; ready: SLO high
        BT      busy
        LDI     r7, 0                   ; status
        LDI     r4, 0
        LDI     r5, 0
        LDI     r3, 16
        LDSR    r4                      ; SR = 0: a short first chunk is
        CRCI                            ; right-aligned; CRC = 0
        MOV     r6, r1
        ANDI    r6, 63                  ; n
        LDI     r2, 2                   ; BiSS ...
        MOV     r0, r1
        SHLI    r0, 8                   ; C = bit 8: SSI
        BNC     rbiss
        LDI     r2, 1                   ; ... or SSI
        LDIH    r7, 0x80                ; status bit 15
        JMP     rtot
rbiss:  ADDI    r6, 8                   ; nE, nW and the CRC follow the data
rtot:   LDI     r0, 0                   ; r6 = bits after CDS; split into
        CMPI    r6, 33                  ; chunks of 16 from the end: the
        BNC     rle32                   ; first chunk's length goes to CNT,
        SUBI    r6, 32                  ; T = three chunks, C = two
        LDI     r0, 4
        JMP     rset
rle32:  CMPI    r6, 17
        BNC     rset
        SUBI    r6, 16
        LDI     r0, 2
rset:   CSRW    CNT, r6
        CSRW    FLAGS, r0               ; nothing below changes a flag until
        LDI     r6, NACK                ; the dispatch reads T and C
        SETD    1                       ; anchor, and wait for that tick, so
        WAITD   0                       ; the first fall is staged after it and
        DJNZ    r2, ack                 ; lands on the next; 2: BiSS (r2 is now 1)

; SSI: one pulse with no sample (its falling edge latches the position, its
; rising edge puts the MSB on SLO), then the chunks.
ssi:    SETP    MA, 0, D
        WAITD   1                       ; fall 1: the encoder latches
        SETP    MA, 1, D
        WAITD   1                       ; rise 1: the MSB goes out
        BT      c1                      ; three chunks
        BC      c2                      ; two
        JMP     c3                      ; one

; BiSS-C: clock until a sample finds the Ack (SLO low), then until one
; finds the start bit (SLO high); the next sample is CDS.
ack:    SETP    MA, 0, D
        WAITD   1                       ; fall
        JP      SLO, 0, acked           ; sample: Ack?
        SETP    MA, 1, D
        WAITD   1                       ; rise
        DJNZ    r6, ack
        JMP     noack
acked:  SETP    MA, 1, D
        WAITD   1
        MOV     r0, r6                  ; NACK + 1 - (the sample that saw it)
        LDI     r6, NSTART
start1: SETP    MA, 0, D
        WAITD   1
        JP      SLO, 1, started         ; sample: start bit?
        SETP    MA, 1, D
        WAITD   1
        DJNZ    r6, start1
        JMP     noack
started: SETP   MA, 1, D
        WAITD   1
        LDI     r6, 0
        SETP    MA, 0, D
        WAITD   1
        JP      SLO, 0, cds0            ; sample: CDS
        LDI     r6, 1
cds0:   SETP    MA, 1, D
        WAITD   1
        BT      c1
        BC      c2
        JMP     c3

; The data: up to three chunks through SR, the CRC unit taking every bit.
; Each bit is a period: SLO sampled after the falling edge.
c1:     SETP    MA, 0, D
        WAITD   1
        SHI
        SETP    MA, 1, D
        WAITD   1
        BNZ     c1
        STSR    r4
        CSRW    CNT, r3                 ; the next chunks are 16 bits
c2:     SETP    MA, 0, D
        WAITD   1
        SHI
        SETP    MA, 1, D
        WAITD   1
        BNZ     c2
        STSR    r5
        CSRW    CNT, r3
c3:     SETP    MA, 0, D
        WAITD   1
        SHI
        SETP    MA, 1, D
        WAITD   1
        BNZ     c3
        STSR    r2                      ; MA stays high: the frame is over
        JMP     post

busy:   LDI     r7, 0x10                ; BUSY (SSI's bit 15 is not set here)
        JMP     report0
noack:  ORI     r7, 0x08                ; NOACK
report0: LDI    r4, 0
        LDI     r5, 0
        JMP     report

; After the frame, no deadline: SLO's chunks are r4, r5, r2 (the last bit
; in r2 bit 0), r0 the Ack counter, r6 CDS.
        .org    0x180                   ; thread 3's quarter (see the header)
post:   MOV     r3, r1
        SHLI    r3, 8                   ; C = bit 8: SSI
        BC      spos
        LDI     r3, NACK - 2            ; periods after the earliest Ack
        SUB     r3, r3, r0              ; (the third sample)
        SHLI    r3, 8
        OR      r7, r7, r3              ; status bits 11:8
        SHLI    r6, 5
        OR      r7, r7, r6              ; status bit 5: CDS
        MOV     r3, r2
        SHLI    r3, 9                   ; C = bit 7: nE
        BC      eok
        ORI     r7, 0x01                ; ERR
eok:    MOV     r3, r2
        SHLI    r3, 10                  ; C = bit 6: nW
        BC      wok
        ORI     r7, 0x02                ; WARN
wok:    STCRC   r3
        MOV16   r6, RESIDUE
        CMP     r3, r6
        BZ      crcok
        ORI     r7, 0x04                ; CRC
crcok:  MOV     r3, r4                  ; the position: r4:r5:r2 >> 8
        SHLI    r3, 8
        MOV     r6, r5
        SHRI    r6, 8
        OR      r4, r3, r6              ; bits 31:16
        SHLI    r5, 8
        SHRI    r2, 8
        OR      r5, r5, r2              ; bits 15:0
        JMP     gray
spos:   MOV     r4, r5                  ; SSI: r5:r2 is the position
        MOV     r5, r2
gray:   MOV     r3, r1
        SHLI    r3, 7                   ; C = bit 9: GRAY
        BNC     report
        MOV     r2, r4
        CALL    g2b
        MOV     r4, r2
        MOV     r2, r5
        CALL    g2b
        MOV     r5, r2
        MOV     r3, r4                  ; the low half also takes the XOR of
        ANDI    r3, 1                   ; every Gray bit above it: bit 0 of
        BZ      report                  ; the decoded high half
        NOT     r5, r5
report: PUSH    r7
        PUSH    r5
        PUSH    r4
        JMP     cmd

; Gray to binary in r2 (16 bits), r6 scratch: each bit becomes the XOR of
; itself and every bit above it.
g2b:    MOV     r6, r2
        SHRI    r6, 1
        XOR     r2, r2, r6
        MOV     r6, r2
        SHRI    r6, 2
        XOR     r2, r2, r6
        MOV     r6, r2
        SHRI    r6, 4
        XOR     r2, r2, r6
        MOV     r6, r2
        SHRI    r6, 8
        XOR     r2, r2, r6
        RET
